 all of this raises the  risk that we get this toxic mix of both  inflation from these constraints and  lower production and economic growth  chelsea what does this mean downstream  from these ports as we look at the  retailers who are so dependent on  getting these products what does it mean  for christmas  yeah i hate to be the grinch here but it  does look like it's going to be a bit of  a difficult christmas especially for for  shoppers and consumers we are hearing  more and more from retailers from  supermarkets that that they're having  trouble getting products and that we  could see shortages we've heard this  from supermarkets here in germany over  the past couple of days they say  things like pasta or potatoes or meat  could be in short supply quite quickly  as well there are a lot of goods that  are still stuck in asia so toys  electronics all of these  things that people might be wanting to  buy in the christmas season  it's possible that they may not get here  in time so some big retailers are  looking at other options they're looking  at flying goods over they're looking at  moving production to other facilities  for example closer to them and in latin  america  but this is something that's going to  last a long time and we likely will see  less supply and more expensive goods in  this winter  all right dw financial correspondent  chelsea delaney in frankfort thank you  very much  all right chelsea touched on this a  little bit but with ports tied up why  can't companies just ship over land  well many are trying to do just that and  now demand for truck drivers is rising  quickly now it's a problem that's  especially pronounced in the uk  but it's hitting continental europe as  well  yaroslav fennec has been driving trucks  for 15 years  not an easy job  there's a lot of stress all the time  lots of traffic many people don't behave  very nicely  you lose a lot of time at work and we  work every day 12 13 and sometimes 15  hours  based in belgium he hauls freight all  over europe  demand for workers like him has gone up  over the years and so have the  challenges he faces  many things have to change firstly  parking  then we have a tachograph which records  driving time and the fines are very high  if we break the rules if i don't find a  place to sleep my driving time goes up  and that's a big problem  across the channel in the uk a massive  shortage of drivers has been straining  supply chains the pandemic and brexit  caused many eu drivers to leave  to try and lure them back uk prime  minister boris johnson promised special  visas initially valid till christmas now  extended to next march  dutch trade unionist edwin atema says  that's not nearly enough actually  they're laughing like do they really  think that we quit our eu job to help  the uk until christmas eve yeah that's  short-term solution and which is no  solution a veteran trucker himself atema  is critical of the industry in the uk  but also the eu where large sections of  the trade rely on cheap labor from  eastern europe  in western europe european laws are not  enforced which allows that workers are  exploited these exploited workers fill  up the gaps in the supply chain which  cannot be happened  in the uk in a moment  some fear that down the line continental  europe may also end up with gaps that  cannot be filled even trucking companies  with better working conditions already  struggle to recruit we got trucks we got  trailers we got jobs  but we don't find the drivers  with shortness of drivers  the prices will be higher because  everybody wants to be served and then  the risk is that equality will fall down  britain's crisis could lead to improved  working conditions for drivers there  salaries have already gone up but  yaroslav fennick thinks it will take  more than that to draw eu drivers back  the offer would have to be super extra  mega good to make it interesting  and at least for the moment  it is not  [Music]  all right lars griner is as